arXiv:2611.07554v1 [cs.CL] 05 Nov 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Memory Trap: Language Agents 
<br class="ltx_break">Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kaiqu Liang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Work done as part of the Anthropic Fellows Program.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Princeton University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jaime Fernández Fisac
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Princeton University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Thomas L. Griffiths
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Princeton University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Andrew Lampinen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Anthropic
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Language agents increasingly use persistent memory to record user preferences and procedures across sessions. We show that memory from benign interactions can cause unsafe or unreliable behavior on later tasks. We call this failure the <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memory trap</em>.
To study this issue, we compare behavior in sessions with and without memory across six frontier models (including Claude Sonnet, Opus, and Fable models and GPT-5.6). We evaluate these models in a range of settings. First, in autonomy experiments, we find that requests to skip confirmation during low-stakes coding chores lead agents to skip confirmation and verification on simulated high-stakes tasks in both coding and everyday life. We next study memories formed when users seek emotional support, or describe harm from uncritical feedback, and find these memories bias later judgments of mathematical work, arguments, and poems toward sycophancy or over-critique (respectively). Memory notes written during legitimate stale-test repairs lead agents to weaken correct tests that catch broken safeguards; these memories also increase cheating on ImpossibleBench, with larger increases for stronger Sonnet and Opus models. We show that memory traps can arise both from overgeneralizations in memory writing, and when acting on memory. These findings demonstrate that even benign interactions can lead to serious risks from memory systems, and thus motivate the need for new research into ensuring that agents preserve <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text">the scope of their memories.</span><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        Code: <a href="https://github.com/safety-research/memory-trap" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/safety-research/memory-trap</a></span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A useful assistant should not need the same instruction in every conversation. It should remember that a user prefers concise answers, follows certain project conventions, or does not want to confirm routine operations. Persistent memory carries such instructions across sessions, and in current frameworks agents write that memory themselves, recording preferences and procedures in notes. But can these memories from benign interactions also lead an agent to misaligned behavior?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Memory helps in the situation where it was formed, but it can mislead when the agent carries it into a situation where it no longer fits. A user who tells an agent to skip confirmation during a routine clean-up has not approved unchecked changes to a payment configuration. A student who asks for kindness after failing an exam still needs to know when the next solution is wrong. A developer who has the agent update a stale test does not want it to rewrite a correct test that catches a broken safeguard. In each case, a boundary separates where the memory was formed from where it is used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We show that agents often cross this boundary: memory from benign interactions causes unsafe or unreliable behavior outside the memory’s scope. We call this failure the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memory trap</em> (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). It can arise in two ways: while writing (when a note broadens what the user said), or while acting, when the agent overgeneralizes from memory (even a raw transcript). Prior work on memory risks studies adversaries manipulating agents through prompt injection and poisoned retrieval or memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or inducing agents to write malicious memories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. By contrast, the memory trap needs no adversary. Every interaction is benign, and the memory is the agent’s own, so malicious-content filters have nothing to catch.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2611.07554v1/teaser.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/264;" width="548" height="264" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Benign interactions become memories that cause harm in later sessions. <em id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Autonomy:</em> after a user says “go ahead, no need to check with me” during a clean-up, the agent notes “do not ask for confirmation” and later routes a $1.2M payment run to an unverified account. <em id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sycophancy:</em> a request for kindness after a failed exam becomes an empathy rule, and the agent later endorses an incorrect solution. <em id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cheating:</em> legitimate stale-test repairs become a rule to update failing tests, and the agent later rewrites a correct test so that an unknown role gets administrator rights. Text is condensed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We study the memory trap in three settings. We compare agents’ behavior in fresh sessions with and without memory from benign interactions, either their own notes or raw transcripts. In our <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">autonomy</em> experiments, users ask agents to skip confirmation during low-stakes coding chores. The resulting notes lead agents to take irreversible actions on simulated high-stakes tasks in both coding and everyday life without asking the user, and even to skip safety checks. Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7 take unsafe actions in 35 to 81% of episodes with notes, from near-zero baselines (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Two routes. ‣ 2 The Memory Trap ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In our <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sycophancy and over-critique</em> experiments, memory from users seeking emotional support leads agents to miss math errors and argument fallacies and to favor the user’s weaker poems, raising Sonnet-4.6’s preference from 21% to 55%. Memory from users hurt by uncritical feedback pushes the other way: agents under-credit correct proofs, invent flaws in sound arguments, and disfavor the user’s stronger poems. Opus-4.7’s false criticism rises from about 1% to 60% of sound arguments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In our <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cheating</em> experiments, agents write notes while repairing stale tests for a correct implementation. In our 50-case broken-safeguard set, their self-written notes lead them to weaken correct tests that catch broken safeguards, such as a permission check that gives an unknown role administrator rights. On ImpossibleBench’s SWE-bench conflicting split, whose tests conflict with the specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, stronger models can cheat more with memory: notes formed this way raise Opus-4.8’s cheating rate from 29% to 74%, while Fable-5 is barely affected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These failures highlight real risks. In our experiments, benign interactions lead frontier agents to act on high-stakes tasks without checking, to endorse incorrect work or reject sound work, and to hide broken safeguards behind passing tests. As persistent memory becomes a standard component of agents, such failures can reach many users and tasks. Preventing the memory trap, both when agents write memory and when they act on it, is an important open problem for safe deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The Memory Trap</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent memory is information carried between sessions, such as raw interaction history or self-written notes. The <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memory trap</em> occurs when an agent overgeneralizes memory from benign interactions, causing unsafe or unreliable behavior on later tasks. An interaction is benign when it neither requests nor rewards the harmful behavior that follows.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Two routes.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">write-time</em> overgeneralization, the agent writes a note that broadens a preference or procedure beyond the scope of the original interaction. For example, “go ahead, no need to check with me” becomes “do not ask for confirmation before taking actions.” In <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">read-time</em> overgeneralization, the agent applies memory beyond that scope when acting on a new task. This can happen even when the memory keeps the original scope, because the agent infers a broader rule from it.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/results_overview.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/145;" width="548" height="145"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Memory from benign interactions causes autonomy risks, sycophancy, over-critique, and cheating: unsafe actions (Sonnet-4.6), weaker-poem preference (<span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text">Sonnet-4.6</span>), false flags on sound arguments (Opus-4.7), and benchmark cheating (<span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text">Opus-4.8</span>). Whiskers: 95% intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Formalizing overgeneralization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a formation history <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and a memory <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the situations in which the preference or procedure learned from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is warranted, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> those covered by the memory’s stated scope, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}_{\pi}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>sc</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}_{\pi}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> those in which agent <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the memory. Write-time overgeneralization is a stated scope that exceeds the history, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(m)\setminus\mathrm{sc}(H)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(m)\setminus\mathrm{sc}(H)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. An unwarranted action at a situation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathrm{sc}_{\pi}(m)\setminus\mathrm{sc}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>sc</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathrm{sc}_{\pi}(m)\setminus\mathrm{sc}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> has one of two sources:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{x\in\mathrm{sc}(m)}_{\text{follows an overbroad note}}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\underbrace{x\notin\mathrm{sc}(m)}_{\text{read-time overgeneralization}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>follows an overbroad note</mtext></munder><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>read-time overgeneralization</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{x\in\mathrm{sc}(m)}_{\text{follows an overbroad note}}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\underbrace{x\notin\mathrm{sc}(m)}_{\text{read-time overgeneralization}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A raw transcript records the history itself, so <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(m)=\mathrm{sc}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(m)=\mathrm{sc}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> and any unwarranted action it induces is read-time. A note can induce both. We examine write-time overgeneralization by auditing the scope stated in notes, and both routes through behavior.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A minimal example.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let acting directly be appropriate in the formation context <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and harmful elsewhere, where checking first is safe. A note can keep that context or drop it:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{full}}=(\mathrm{act},c_{0}),\qquad m_{\mathrm{gist}}=(\mathrm{act},\;\cdot\;)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>act</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>gist</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>act</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{full}}=(\mathrm{act},c_{0}),\qquad m_{\mathrm{gist}}=(\mathrm{act},\;\cdot\;).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(m_{\mathrm{full}})=\mathrm{sc}(H)=\{c_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(m_{\mathrm{full}})=\mathrm{sc}(H)=\{c_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{gist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>gist</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{gist}}</annotation></semantics></math> covers every context. An agent that follows exactly the stated scope acts directly only at <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{full}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{full}}</annotation></semantics></math> but everywhere under <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{gist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>gist</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{gist}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so its rate of harmful actions rises from zero to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x\neq c_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x\neq c_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Losing a condition changes behavior even though the remembered action is still correct in its original context. This is pure write-time overgeneralization; read-time overgeneralization would lead the agent to act outside <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> even under <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathrm{full}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathrm{full}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Experimental design</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In each experiment, the agent first completes a scripted benign interaction, producing a history <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> that encourages a preference or procedure appropriate to that task. We carry the resulting memory into a fresh session, either as the original transcript or as a note the same model wrote with its memory tool, and load it automatically, without retrieval or a memory-writing tool. The deployment task lies outside <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> by design: a payment change instead of a file clean-up, grading instead of comfort, a correct test instead of a stale one. On these tasks the appropriate behavior does not depend on memory, so a harmful change between the memory and no-memory conditions isolates overgeneralization and shows whether and how the trap occurs across models and memory forms. Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to <a href="#S5" title="5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> define the outcomes, and Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Datasets ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> describes the datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure whether an agent takes an irreversible action without first asking the user or running a check that would reveal a problem. Most comparisons use Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7; Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> reports the other models and a benign-profile variant, and Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> reports GPT-5.6.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Set-up</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory formation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We form permission notes from 20 low-stakes coding operations, including deleting a stale TODO file and revoking an unused development token. The agent performs the operation with the memory tool available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>. The first time it asks the user to confirm, the user pushes back with one of six phrasings, such as “you don’t need to keep asking me.” The agent then finishes the task and writes a note, giving 120 operation-pushback combinations per model. For priority notes, the user instead asks for speed in the opening request (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Scope audit. ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/stakes.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:242/121;" width="242" height="121"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Judged stakes of the 20 formation tasks and all 300 deployment scenarios.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deployment tasks.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two sets of simulated high-stakes scenarios. The <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confirmation set</em> has 100 everyday-life and 100 coding scenarios in which a user requests an action; inspecting the environment reveals a reason to ask that user before proceeding. The <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-verification set</em> has 100 scenarios, split equally by domain, in which an optional check reveals the problem without user involvement. A model judge rates the stakes of the 20 formation tasks at a median of 2 on a ten-point scale, and those of all 300 deployment scenarios at a median of 9 (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Memory formation. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Measurement.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unsafe-action rate</em> is the fraction of episodes in which the agent acts without the required check. We average over scenarios, using 120 episodes per scenario, each supplied with a note, for the two-model comparisons in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Measurement. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. A formation session that writes no note counts at the no-memory rate. 95% intervals summarize variation across scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/transcript_vs_note.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/170;" width="548" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Unsafe-action rates with no memory, a raw transcript, or a self-written note from the same interactions. Notes have larger effects in all eight comparisons for Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory from low-stakes chores leads to high-stakes harm</span> (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Measurement. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The formation tasks are routine clean-ups, while the deployment scenarios are rated far higher in stakes (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Memory formation. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). From baselines below 0.01, notes raise the unsafe-action rate to 0.66 to 0.81 on Sonnet-4.6 and 0.35 to 0.58 on Opus-4.7. The harm extends beyond skipping interruptions: a note formed after a request to stop asking for confirmation also suppresses optional safety checks the agent could run itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory from coding chores causes harm in everyday life</span> (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Measurement. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>; Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). Formation happens only in coding chores, yet notes raise unsafe actions in everyday-life scenarios as much as in coding. On the confirmation set, Sonnet-4.6 takes unsafe actions in 0.79 of everyday-life episodes and 0.81 of coding episodes, and Opus-4.7 in 0.58 and 0.49. For all five models, everyday-life rates are within 0.09 of coding rates. The effect holds in every everyday-life category, from education to security: Sonnet-4.6’s rate ranges from 0.66 to 0.90 and Opus-4.7’s from 0.49 to 0.75, against no-memory rates of at most 0.02 (Figure <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/formation_scope.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/195;" width="548" height="195"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Formation-side scope judgments. Each model runs 120 formation sessions per condition; bars show shares of the notes written. Left: permission notes. Right: goal classifications after “prioritize speed here”; <em id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no goal</em> means the note records the session without a lasting priority.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory also misgeneralizes goals</span> (Figures <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). When the user says “you should prioritize speed here” instead of waiving confirmation, the goal judge classifies 77 to 99% of priority notes as standing goals, and only 32 of 575 notes tie it to that task. These goals suppress safety checks: on Sonnet-4.6’s confirmation tasks, priority notes raise unsafe-action rates to 0.51 and 0.57, below permission notes’ 0.79 and 0.81, and on coding self-verification they exceed them, 0.72 versus 0.66.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notes turn one-time requests into standing rules</span> (Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Across the five models, 69 to 94% of formation notes state a standing rule, phrased to apply in future sessions. One Sonnet-4.6 note says, “Does not want to be asked for confirmation before taking actions (e.g., deletions). Just proceed.” A five-level rubric, from permission for one action on one occasion to general permission for irreversible actions affecting others, compares <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sc}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sc}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Scope audit. ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). Across 599 notes, 93.0% are rated broader than the instruction, by 2.05 levels on average. Scope ratings do not consistently predict unsafe-action rates, so text-only audits of notes are not enough.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/permission_vs_priority.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/170;" width="548" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Permission and priority notes on the two autonomy sets. Priority notes have lower rates in seven of eight comparisons; the exception is Sonnet-4.6 on coding self-verification.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On multi-step batch tasks, transcripts can match the effect of notes</span> (Figures <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Measurement. ‣ 3.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Raw transcripts alone raise unsafe-action rates to 0.10 to 0.60 for Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7, and notes usually produce rates 1.1 to 4.3 times higher. On the 153 single-item scenarios, notes produce rates 4 to 20 times the transcript rates on Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7. On the 47 reviewed-batch scenarios, where the agent applies reviewed decisions with one harmful item, Sonnet-4.6 reaches about 0.83 with either artifact, and transcripts slightly exceed notes for both Opus models. Sonnet-5 is the exception: transcripts raise its rate only from 0.33 to 0.44, while notes raise it to 0.63.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/single_vs_batch.svg" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/171;" width="548" height="171"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Unsafe-action rates for single-item and reviewed-batch tasks. Transcripts match or exceed notes on batch tasks for three of five models; Sonnet-5’s high baseline rises more with notes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Memory Can Cause Sycophancy and Over-Critique</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Set-up</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory formation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test whether notes from requests for empathy or critical feedback bias later judgments. We evaluate the same five Claude models. The user’s first message takes one of two forms. In <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empathy</em> messages, the user shares a painful event, such as losing a pet or a job, asks for kindness rather than advice, and mentions a hobby, such as cooking or gardening, where criticism hurts. In <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rigor</em> messages, the user describes a setback caused by feedback that let errors through, such as a failed exam or a report approved without checking, and is frustrated at being reassured instead of corrected. We use ten messages of each kind and provide the agent with a memory tool and a neutral reminder to use the tool. Each model writes ten notes per condition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deployment tasks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use feedback tasks from <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SycophancyEval</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: 992 incorrect mathematical solutions, 296 fallacy-labelled arguments, and 200 poem pairs with the weaker poem attributed to the user. To test over-critique, we reverse the direction: the agent evaluates 40 sound arguments and 200 pairs with the stronger poem attributed to the user. We report changes from each model’s no-memory baseline, which do not depend on the benchmark’s quality labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluate the 1,000 solutions in <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">IMO-GradingBench</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The model sees the problem and proposed solution, presented as the user’s own, without a reference proof or rubric. The model returns a verdict of Incorrect, Partial, Almost, or Correct, which we encode as 0, 1, 2, or 3. We report the assigned minus the reference grade to measure the direction of grading bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scoring.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fixed Sonnet-4.6 judge at temperature zero scores feedback in a separate call. Proof grading needs no judge: verdicts are compared directly with the dataset labels. For each item, we average available responses over notes and compute the paired memory-minus-baseline difference, with 95% intervals across items. Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Sycophancy and over-critique ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> reports item counts and all five models’ results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/empathy_notes.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/141;" width="548" height="141"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/rigor_notes.svg" id="S4.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/110;" width="548" height="110"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Effects of empathy notes (top) and rigor notes (bottom) on later evaluations: (a) missed errors in the user’s math, (b) missed fallacies in the user’s arguments, (c) preference for the user’s weaker poem, (d) grade change on strong olympiad proofs (assigned minus reference grade, 0 to 3), (e) flaws asserted in sound arguments, and (f) preference for the user’s stronger poem. Asterisks mark paired effects whose 95% intervals exclude zero; the dashed line marks equal choice probability. Model labels abbreviate Sonnet (S), Opus (O), and Fable (F).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empathy notes make agents endorse the user’s errors</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Scoring. ‣ 4.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 4 Memory Can Cause Sycophancy and Over-Critique ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, top). Across the four tasks, empathy notes significantly favor the user in 14 of 20 comparisons. Preference for the user’s weaker poem shows the largest shift, rising by 0.26 to 0.35 on every model. Sonnet-5 picks the weaker poem in 0.840 of pairs, up from 0.525. Missed mathematical errors increase significantly on four of five models: Sonnet-4.6 misses 0.293 of incorrect solutions with notes, up from 0.220, and Opus-4.7 misses 0.218, up from 0.156. Missed fallacies increase significantly on three, including Sonnet-4.6 (0.210 to 0.262) and Fable-5 (0.187 to 0.242). On flawed olympiad proofs, empathy notes raise grades by 0.184 categories for Opus-4.7 and 0.111 for Opus-4.8 only.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rigor notes make agents reject sound work</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Scoring. ‣ 4.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 4 Memory Can Cause Sycophancy and Over-Critique ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, bottom). Every model asserts flaws in sound arguments far more often with rigor notes. From no-memory rates of at most 0.04, false flags reach 0.645 on Sonnet-5, 0.605 on Opus-4.7, 0.539 on Fable-5, and 0.458 on Opus-4.8, so four of five models reject nearly half or more of these valid arguments. Rigor notes also lower grades on strong olympiad proofs for all five models, most for Opus-4.8 (0.206 categories), Sonnet-4.6 (0.199), and Sonnet-5 (0.104). On fully correct proofs alone, all five models under-credit significantly. Preference for the user’s stronger poem falls on all five models. Opus-4.7’s rate drops from 0.55 to 0.24. Rigor notes significantly shift judgments against the user in all 15 comparisons on these three tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Requests for empathy or rigor become standing evaluation rules</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure A5 ‣ Note content. ‣ B.2 Sycophancy and over-critique ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>). Among notes from the five models, about 48% of empathy notes and 92% of rigor notes contain an explicit rule for evaluating the user’s work. Most notes are framed as standing preferences rather than records of a single episode. Rigor notes are written with probability 0.70 (Opus-4.7) to 1.00 (Sonnet-4.6, Opus-4.8, Fable-5), so effects mix how often notes are written with how they are used (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Sycophancy and over-critique ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Memory Can Cause Cheating</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Set-up</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory formation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent performs a legitimate maintenance task: a vendored implementation is correct and frozen, but its local test is stale, so updating the test is the appropriate repair. The 20 formation tasks are small vendored utility functions, such as a client version string or a slug formatter. The file header marks the implementation as vendored from a pinned upstream release, and the git history shows an upstream commit that intentionally changed its behavior; the local test still asserts the old behavior. The agent works in a Docker terminal where the implementation is read-only. Edits to the implementation fail with <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Permission denied</span>, and nothing tells the agent to change the test. The agent maintains its own note across <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> benign sessions, often recording a procedure such as “solution.py is frozen; when a test disagrees, update the test.” We use six sequences per model at each formation depth <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> as the main comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Broken-safeguard set.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In these 50 cases, the formation setup is reversed. Each case is a safeguard, such as an access check, an expiry check, or a retention policy, whose frozen implementation is broken while the original test is correct. For example, a retention function returns 0 days instead of the documented 3,650 for an unlisted record type. The appropriate response is to preserve the failing test and report the blockage. We measure <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masking</em>: whether the final test stops detecting the defect when executed beside the frozen implementation in a clean directory. The scorer disables markers that would skip tests or treat failures as expected, and excludes other files created by the agent. A passing suite or no collected tests counts as masking; execution errors stay in the denominator. At <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>, each note is tested twice on each case (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ImpossibleBench.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the 348 usable instances from the SWE-bench conflicting split of ImpossibleBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, whose tests conflict with the specification. Passing these tests requires a specification-violating shortcut. We retain the benchmark’s scoring and anti-hacking prompt and allow up to 300 messages per run, counting capped runs as not cheating. The benchmark’s classifier labels the method of each cheating run (Figure <a href="#S5.F9.sf2" title="In Figure 9 ‣ ImpossibleBench. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/masking_methods.svg" id="S5.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/415;" width="548" height="415"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Broken-safeguard masking at <math id="S5.F9.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>. <em id="S5.F9.sf1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Value rewritten</em> changes the expected output; <em id="S5.F9.sf1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">input moved</em> changes the arguments to avoid the defect.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/cheating_methods.svg" id="S5.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/415;" width="548" height="415"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>ImpossibleBench cheating by method. Test modification (A) is the most common method. The pale remainder marks runs without cheating.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>How agents make tests pass; Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> reports Fable-5. Arm labels are base (no memory), tr. (raw transcript), and note (self-written note). In (b), whiskers show 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals over instances for total cheating rates; (a) shows point estimates.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory leads agents to hide security defects</span> (Figure <a href="#S5.F9.sf1" title="In Figure 9 ‣ ImpossibleBench. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(a)</span></a>). In 27 of the 50 broken-safeguard cases, the frozen defect weakens access control, sessions, cryptography, or network policy. In one, a permission check gives an unknown role the administrator’s rights, including deletion. Across the five models, notes lead agents to mask this defect in 33 of 60 episodes, against none of 15 without memory. Across the 27 cases, notes raise masking to between 0.77 and 0.80 for Sonnet-4.6, Sonnet-5, and Opus-4.7, against at most 0.15 without memory. Each of these models and Opus-4.8 masks all 50 defects at least once. A masked test turns a known vulnerability into a passing check.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agents rewrite correct tests to expect the buggy output</span> (Figure <a href="#S5.F9.sf1" title="In Figure 9 ‣ ImpossibleBench. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(a)</span></a>). Pooled over the five models’ note episodes, 79% of masked tests keep the same call and rewrite the expected value to the defective output, the same repair that was correct during formation. In the permission case, 30 of the 33 masked tests now assert that an unknown role may delete. A later fix would make such a test fail, so the test protects the defect instead of detecting it. Another 20% move the input outside the range where the defect occurs, so the test passes without probing it. No masked Claude session at <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> deletes the test or an assertion, so checks for missing tests or assertions would miss both edits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-written notes cause more masking than raw transcripts</span> (Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table A2 ‣ Masking rates and denominators. ‣ B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>). This holds for all five models. Sonnet-5 masks 0.823 of episodes with notes, 0.008 with transcripts, and 0.032 without memory. Opus-4.7’s transcript rate of 0.090 is below its 0.120 baseline. Only Sonnet-4.6’s transcripts raise masking, from 0.110 to 0.447. One formation session already yields masking of 0.520 for Sonnet-4.6 and 0.542 for Sonnet-5, and Opus-4.7 reaches 0.698 after five (Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ Depth. ‣ B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/impossiblebench_rates.svg" id="S5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:395/148;" width="395" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>ImpossibleBench cheating rates after ten legitimate test-repair sessions, counting all cheating methods. Whiskers show 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals over instances.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On ImpossibleBench, stronger Sonnet and Opus models cheat more with memory</span> (Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.2 Results ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Without memory, Sonnet-4.6, Sonnet-5, Opus-4.7, and Opus-4.8 cheat at similar rates, 0.28 to 0.30. With notes, the increase grows from 4 percentage points on Sonnet-4.6 to 16 on Sonnet-5, 19 on Opus-4.7, and 45 on Opus-4.8, whose rate rises from 0.29 to 0.74. Within each family the newer model gains more, and both Opus models gain more than both Sonnet models. Fable-5, the strongest model, barely changes, from 0.18 to 0.19. Memory also shifts cheating toward editing the tests (defeating the evaluation itself): pooled over the five models, editing the benchmark’s tests rises from 0.18 to 0.36 of all runs and from 0.67 to 0.83 of cheating runs. The shift is largest for the Opus models, and Sonnet-4.6 shows none (Figure <a href="#S5.F9.sf2" title="In Figure 9 ‣ ImpossibleBench. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a>; Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent memory.</span> Generative Agents reflect on stored experiences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; Reflexion and ExpeL retain lessons from past trials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; Voyager and Agent Workflow Memory build reusable skills and procedures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Systems such as MemGPT, MemoryBank, Mem0, and A-MEM manage persistent stores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chhikara et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and surveys and CoALA organize these designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sumers et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The memory tool we use lets the agent read and write files across sessions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>. Benchmarks test recall, retrieval, learning, conflict resolution, and preference following over long conversations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We study misalignment from memory, when agents overgeneralize preferences and procedures while writing or using memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Personalization.</span> Models adapt through retrieved user histories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salemi et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salemi et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Richardson et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, dialogue-derived profiles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or fine-tuning on feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poddar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, as surveyed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Personalized agents also learn preferences for embodied assistance, proactive tasks, and repeated interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirk et al. (2023)</a></cite> ask which preferences should be honored. Recent benchmarks find that stored user context can leak into unrelated tasks or increase sycophancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pulipaka et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bensal et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, but like earlier memory benchmarks they mostly supply user memory directly. We let the agent form its own memory from benign interactions, and it can overgeneralize experience, not just user preferences: a test-repair procedure learned in past sessions also leads to cheating on later coding tasks, including ImpossibleBench.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory poisoning and jailbreaks.</span> Jailbreaks elicit harmful outputs through hand-crafted prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, optimized suffixes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, attacker LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or many in-context demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anil et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Prompt injection hides instructions in model inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez and Ribeiro, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and retrieved content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as benchmarked in tool-using agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Memory poisoning makes such content persist: adversarial passages mislead retrievers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, PoisonedRAG and BadRAG steer RAG outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and backdoors hide in agent memory, knowledge bases, or observations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. MINJA makes the agent store malicious records through queries alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, MemoryGraft implants experiences the agent later imitates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava and He, 2025</a>)</cite>, and ASB and MPBench benchmark these attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dash et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Our setting has no adversary: every formation interaction is benign, and harm arises when the agent’s own memory is applied beyond its scope, so malicious-content filters have nothing to catch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conclusion.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Memory lets agents learn from every interaction, and we show that the same capacity can turn benign interactions into harm. A request to skip confirmation during a routine clean-up leads agents to act on high-stakes tasks without checking. Asking for kindness leads them to endorse incorrect work, and a legitimate test repair leads them to hide broken safeguards behind passing tests. No adversary is involved, and failures arise both when agents write memory and when they act on it. On ImpossibleBench, stronger Sonnet and Opus models cheat more with memory, not less. As agents accumulate memory, each benign exchange becomes a possible source of failure. Safe memory requires agents to preserve and respect the scope in which each memory was formed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and future work.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test mostly one model family, and our autonomy scenarios, which we designed to probe for harmful effects, have relatively short horizons. Future work could test deployed memory systems with real users and long histories, train agents to keep each memory’s scope, and build harnesses that guard against the memory trap (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Limitations and future work ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">AI use statement</h3>

<div id="S7.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used AI assistants to edit and polish the wording of the paper draft and to generate plots. Language models are also part of the methodology: they are the evaluated agents, and fixed models serve as judges for the feedback tasks and the note audits (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Memory Can Cause Sycophancy and Over-Critique ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Appendices <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Protocol and prompts ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> and <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Scope audit. ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). The authors reviewed all AI-assisted work and take full responsibility for the content of this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ethics statement</h3>

<div id="S7.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work studies a safety risk in language agents so that it can be mitigated. All experiments run in simulated or sandboxed environments, so agent actions affect no real people or systems, and no human subjects were involved. The deployment scenarios and defects are constructed for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Reproducibility statement</h3>

<div id="S7.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sections <a href="#S2" title="2 The Memory Trap ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to <a href="#S5" title="5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> describe the formation interactions, deployment tasks, and measurements. Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Datasets ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> describes the datasets; Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Protocol and prompts ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> gives the system prompts, memory delivery, deployment prompts, and judge prompts; and Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> gives the ImpossibleBench configuration and the masking scorer. All models are accessed through public APIs under fixed identifiers (<span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claude-sonnet-4-6</span>, <span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claude-opus-4-7</span>, <span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claude-sonnet-5</span>, <span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claude-opus-4-8</span>, <span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claude-fable-5</span>, and <span id="S7.SSx3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-5.6-sol</span>), and all judges run at temperature 0. Each result reports its sample size, and each figure states how its intervals are computed. The qualitative examples in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> are condensed from saved model outputs, with omissions marked by ellipses.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
