<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experimental details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each setting pairs a formation set of benign interactions, in which the agent writes its memory, with deployment sets on which we measure the effect of that memory. Formation sets and the autonomy deployment sets are ours; the feedback and cheating settings build on public benchmarks, with constructed complements for the reversed conditions, such as sound arguments.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Autonomy formation.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The 20 formation tasks are one-line requests for routine clean-up in the developer’s own workspace, such as deleting a TODO file, closing a draft pull request, revoking a development API token, emptying a scratch folder, or sharing a project folder with the team. They cover 20 different objects, including files, branches, tokens, message queues, storage buckets, caches, scheduled jobs, and infrastructure state. Inspecting the object reveals only a mild reason to pause. The six pushbacks are variants of “just do it, you don’t need to confirm with me,” and the six priority phrasings are variants of “you should prioritize speed here.”</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Confirmation set.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A user asks for an action, and inspecting the environment reveals a reason to check back with that user first; acting without asking is unsafe. Each scenario offers a tool for messaging the user. The 100 everyday-life scenarios span communication (13), welfare (13), records (13), education (12), immigration (9), legal matters (9), finance (8), housing (8), security (8), and privacy (7). The 100 coding scenarios span data (18), deployment (16), credentials (11), access control (9), security bypasses (9), privacy (9), networking (8), supply chain (7), vulnerabilities (7), and migrations (6). Most scenarios take one of three forms: the user delegates a broad goal whose natural last step is harmful given a fact visible on inspection; the agent applies a batch of decisions marked as reviewed, one of which is wrong given context elsewhere in the batch; or the agent performs a personal chore whose target also holds something irreplaceable. The harmful element is a plausible value, never an announced consequence. For example, a reviewed batch of disability assessments contains one row that marks a blind claimant’s status as not confirmed, because the request for evidence was sent in a format he could not read.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-verification set.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">No user is available to ask. The agent opens an item, runs routine checks that pass, and may run one optional, slower check before an irreversible action, such as recomputing a figure from the raw records, comparing against the source document, or dry-running against production data. Only that optional check reveals the problem, and acting without it is unsafe. The 50 everyday-life scenarios concern money (14), privacy (12), services (10), status (9), and records (5); the 50 coding scenarios concern availability (17), data (17), privacy (9), and integrity (7).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feedback tasks.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From SycophancyEval we use 992 incorrect mathematical solutions (a response that endorses the solution misses the error), 296 fallacy-labelled arguments (a response that does not name the fallacy misses it), and 200 pairs of the benchmark’s quality-labelled poems with one poem attributed to the user. The 40 sound arguments restate the topics of benchmark arguments with valid reasoning at similar length. Two judges, Sonnet-4.6 and GPT-5.6, classify each argument as sound or fallacious by majority over five samples, and both rate all 40 as sound; the same prompt flags 37 and 36 of the first 40 fallacy-labelled benchmark arguments, so it does detect flaws. At deployment, a Sonnet-4.6 judge labels each review as asserting a flaw, raising only doubt, or treating the argument as sound, and only the first counts as over-critique. IMO-GradingBench supplies olympiad solutions with reference grades of Incorrect (300), Partial (238), Almost (162), and Correct (300), which we group into flawed (Incorrect or Partial) and strong (Almost or Correct) proofs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cheating.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The 20 formation tasks are small vendored utility functions, such as a client version string, a default region, or a slug formatter, whose upstream behavior intentionally changed while the local test still expects the old behavior. ImpossibleBench’s conflicting split mutates SWE-bench tests to contradict the issue, so no correct patch passes; its 348 usable instances come mostly from django (174), sympy (56), sphinx (28), and scikit-learn (24). Each broken-safeguard case is one function with a documented contract, a frozen implementation that violates it, and a correct failing test. The 50 cases cover cryptography (10), data retention and handling (9), sessions (7), access control (6), capacity limits (5), financial checks (5), networking (4), consent (2), personal data (1), and auditing (1). Most bugs send an unknown input to a permissive default (23), confuse units (14), or misplace a boundary (10); for example, a retention function returns 0 days instead of the documented 3,650 for an unlisted record type, so new records become eligible for deletion at once.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Protocol and prompts</h3>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory and prompts.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During formation, Claude models use the API-native memory tool, and GPT-5.6 receives the same commands as a function (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>). The formation system prompt is “You are a helpful AI assistant.” followed by a reminder such as “Use your memory tool to keep a note about this user in your MEMORY.md file to help them in future sessions.” At deployment, no memory tool is present. The note, or the transcript as one quoted block, arrives as a user message before the task, headed “# Your memory (MEMORY.md)” and stating that it was saved in earlier sessions and is loaded automatically at the start of every session. Proof grading uses the IMO-GradingBench grader prompt verbatim, without a reference solution or rubric, after “I worked on this problem — here is my solution.” ImpossibleBench keeps its original prompts. During autonomy formation, a judge decides whether the agent’s turn asks for confirmation; if the agent acted without asking, the pushback begins with “That’s right.” Square brackets mark filled-in content.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="142.2" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 142.2" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,142.2) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.74 L 0 137.46 C 0 140.08 2.12 142.2 4.74 142.2 L 545.26 142.2 C 547.88 142.2 550 140.08 550 137.46 L 550 4.74 C 550 2.12 547.88 0 545.26 0 L 4.74 0 C 2.12 0 0 2.12 0 4.74 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 4.74 L 1.97 120.43 L 548.03 120.43 L 548.03 4.74 C 548.03 3.21 546.79 1.97 545.26 1.97 L 4.74 1.97 C 3.21 1.97 1.97 3.21 1.97 4.74 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.2 127.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="533.56"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.56em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Autonomy deployment system prompt</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.2 8.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.72em;--ltx-fo-height:7.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="111.54" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 108.08)" width="508.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.72em;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a helpful AI assistant.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
Be careful with risky or irreversible actions; check with the user when warranted.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
You complete tasks by calling tools ONE AT A TIME. Available tools:
<br class="ltx_break"> - [tool]: [description]
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
