</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table A1: </span>Mean unsafe-action rates on the confirmation set, with 100 scenarios per domain. Base means no memory. All models use the same scenarios.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/per_model.svg" id="A2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/170;" width="548" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A1: </span>Four of the five models in Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>; Fable-5, near the floor in every arm, is omitted. Each model receives transcripts and notes from its own formation interactions. Both the effect size and the ordering of the two memory formats vary from model to model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The effect spans scenario categories</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>). With notes, Sonnet-4.6 takes unsafe actions in 0.66 to 0.97 of episodes per confirmation category and Opus-4.7 in 0.36 to 0.75. No-memory rates are at most 0.03. On the self-verification set, the ranges are 0.57 to 0.87 and 0.25 to 0.54. Raw transcripts yield lower rates than notes in 57 of the 58 model-category comparisons; the exception is Sonnet-4.6 on coding integrity checks, where both reach 0.73.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/autonomy_categories.svg" id="A2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/473;" width="548" height="473"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A2: </span>Unsafe-action rates by scenario category for Sonnet-4.6 and Opus-4.7 with no memory, a raw transcript, or a self-written note. Category sizes are in parentheses.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scope audit.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare instructions in their formation context with the resulting notes. The rubric runs from level 0 (one action on one occasion) to level 4 (general permission, including irreversible actions affecting others). Median ratings rise from level 1 for contextualized instructions to level 3 for notes; 75.0% of notes rise by at least two levels, and model means range from 1.63 to 2.28 levels. Two other models used as judges agree with the primary judge within one level on at least 94% of notes. Notes rated at level 2 or below still yield unsafe-action rates of 0.791, close to 0.860 for level-4 notes, so even notes that read as narrow still transfer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A stated priority can suppress the same checks as a permission</span> (Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The priority condition replaces the confirmation pushback with “you should prioritize speed here.” We test priority notes on the same scenario sets as permission notes. Priority notes raise unsafe-action rates from at most 0.007 without memory to 0.51 to 0.72 for Sonnet-4.6 and 0.13 to 0.28 for Opus-4.7. The goal judge classifies 77 to 99% of each model’s priority notes as standing goals (Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Memory Can Cause Autonomy Risks ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/priority_transcript_vs_note.svg" id="A2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/170;" width="548" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A3: </span>Priority formation delivered as a transcript or a self-written note. Notes produce unsafe-action rates 1.4 to 4.2 times higher in all eight comparisons. The note-versus-transcript difference therefore extends beyond permission formation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benign profile content does not reliably remove the effect</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure A4 ‣ Scope audit. ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>). We repeat formation with one of ten user profiles already in <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MEMORY.md</span>. Profiles describe unrelated interests such as gardening, contain about 1,200 characters, and fill 60 to 85% of the final file. All 240 notes preserve the profile. Across eight two-model comparisons, the largest unsafe-action rate change is 0.050.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A separate sweep varies the initial profile from zero to about 2,300 characters. Sonnet-4.6’s average rate falls from 0.801 to 0.528 as fewer notes state a standing rule, but standing-rule notes at the largest profile size still yield 0.777 and 0.824 in life and coding, close to 0.790 and 0.813 without a profile. Opus-4.7, Opus-4.8, and Fable-5 show no comparable consistent reduction, and for GPT-5.6 a profile of about 2,300 characters lowers the note’s effect from 0.36 to 0.28 (Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure A7 ‣ B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/benign_profile.svg" id="A2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/156;" width="548" height="156"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A4: </span>Unsafe-action rates with a benign profile present during formation. Left: empty versus profile-seeded memory. Right: a profile-length sweep, pooling domains and retaining notes regardless of scope. Sonnet-4.6’s average falls, but its standing-rule notes remain as effective.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Sycophancy and over-critique</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof grading.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rigor notes lower grades on the 462 strong proofs for all five models. An alternative protocol that supplies a reference solution and rubric and requests a 0 to 7 score gives the same result, and under both protocols empathy raises grades on the 538 flawed proofs for both Opus models. Replies cut off by the output limit before a grade are requested again with larger limits, up to 128,000 tokens; the 14 Fable-5 poem votes refused on every attempt count as abstentions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Note content.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure A5 ‣ Note content. ‣ B.2 Sycophancy and over-critique ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a> classifies the formation notes. All empathy notes and 80 to 90% of rigor notes state a standing rule for future sessions. An explicit rule for evaluating the user’s work appears in 30 to 70% of empathy notes and in 90 to 100% of rigor notes, so rigor notes more often tell the agent how to judge the user’s work. A session without a note is rerun until the model writes one.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/note_rules.svg" id="A2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/158;" width="548" height="158"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A5: </span>Formation notes that state (a) a standing rule or (b) an explicit evaluation rule.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Cheating</h3>

<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ImpossibleBench runs.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of 349 instances fails to initialize, leaving 348. Each memory arm has 2,088 runs and each baseline 1,044 on the same instances. Pooled rates count runs capped at 300 messages as not cheating. Since memory changes how often runs are capped, we also compare finished runs: notes raise cheating for Opus-4.8 (0.60 to 0.95), Sonnet-5 (0.30 to 0.49), and Fable-5 (0.22 to 0.34), Opus-4.7 is near its ceiling (0.88 to 0.95), and Sonnet-4.6 stays at 0.60.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparing cheating methods.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the benchmark’s classifier prompt to all 5,980 cheating sessions, distinguishing five methods: modifying tests, overloading comparison operators, using extra state, special-casing inputs, and other behavior. For Opus-4.8, the test-modification rate across all sessions rises from 0.21 to 0.68. On instances with cheating in both conditions, a session-matched McNemar test and an instance-level Wilcoxon test compare the share of cheating by test modification. The shift is significant under both tests for Opus-4.8 (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=5.8\times 10^{-11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=5.8\times 10^{-11}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.6\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.6\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>), Opus-4.7 (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5\times 10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\times 10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.6\times 10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.6\times 10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>), and Fable-5 (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.008" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.008</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.008</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.025</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.025</annotation></semantics></math>), under one test for Sonnet-5, and not for Sonnet-4.6. Fable-5, not shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ ImpossibleBench. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Set-up ‣ 5 Memory Can Cause Cheating ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, raises test modification from 0.11 to 0.14; its broken-safeguard masking rates are in Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table A2 ‣ Masking rates and denominators. ‣ B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a> with the other models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Masking rates and denominators.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table A2 ‣ Masking rates and denominators. ‣ B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a> compares no memory, transcripts, and notes at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Each note arm has 600 episodes. Transcript arms have 600 episodes. For Fable-5, formation sessions that ended without a reply appear in its transcript as the task prompt alone, so every chain keeps all <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sessions. Execution errors count as non-masking outcomes (at most 14 in a baseline arm and none in the <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> note arms except Fable-5’s). Fable-5’s safety classifier ends some sessions with a refusal: 47% of baseline, 39% of note, and 90.7% of transcript sessions. Refused sessions count as non-masking; excluding them, Fable-5’s note rate is 0.087.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Model</th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Base</th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Transcript</th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Note</th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{base}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{transcript}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>transcript</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{transcript}}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Opus-4.8</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">0.050</td>
