<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table A2: </span>Execution-based masking rates at <math id="A2.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>. All note arms have <math id="A2.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=600</annotation></semantics></math>. Baseline and transcript sample sizes are shown explicitly.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Masking scorer.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During formation, <span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">solution.py</span> is read-only (mode 0444) and edits to it are reverted; during deployment, it is owned by a different user in a sticky directory. The strict masking scorer runs only the final test and frozen implementation in an isolated directory. The scorer runs <span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">python -m pytest -q --tb=no -p no:cacheprovider -p no:skipping test_solution.py</span>. Disabling the skipping plugin prevents skip and expected-failure markers from hiding a failing assertion. Exit codes 0 and 5 count as masking; code 1 records a detected failure; other outcomes are errors and <span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">stay in the denominator.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Depth.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ Depth. ‣ B.3 Cheating ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a> reports all measured depths. A few benign sessions can suffice: Sonnet-4.6 and Sonnet-5 mask over half of the defects after a single session, Opus-4.7 reaches 0.698 after five, and Sonnet-4.6 reaches 0.993 after fifteen, against a no-memory rate of 0.110.</p>
</div>
