</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Additional model results</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5.6 goes through the same formation and deployment procedures as the Claude models. The API-native memory tool is Anthropic-specific, so GPT-5.6 receives the same six memory commands and protocol text as an explicit function definition, backed by the same file store.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/gpt56_overview.svg" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/160;" width="548" height="160"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A6: </span>GPT-5.6 in the four settings. (a) Unsafe-action rates on the confirmation and self-verification sets. (b) Empathy notes: missed math errors, missed argument fallacies, and preference for the user’s weaker poem. (c) Rigor notes: flaws asserted in sound arguments and preference for the user’s stronger poem. (d) Grade bias, assigned minus reference grade, on flawed proofs with empathy notes and strong proofs with rigor notes. (e) ImpossibleBench cheating rate and broken-safeguard masking at <math id="A2.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Whiskers show 95% intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notes raise failures in autonomy, sycophancy, and the broken-safeguard set</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure A6 ‣ B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a>). GPT-5.6 has higher no-memory rates than the Claude models on several tasks, so each comparison is against its own baseline. Notes raise unsafe-action rates in all four autonomy comparisons, with raw transcripts in between on the confirmation set. Empathy notes raise preference for the user’s weaker poem from 0.65 to 0.93 and slightly raise missed fallacies. Notes raise broken-safeguard masking from 0.36 to 0.96. Rigor notes move all three over-critique measures in the expected direction, though only slightly, and GPT-5.6 declines to finish over 96% of ImpossibleBench runs in both conditions, leaving too few completed runs to compare the two conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5.6 also writes narrower notes. Only 15% of its permission notes state a standing rule, against 69 to 94% for the Claude models, and 22% of its priority notes tie the priority to that task, against 6% for the Claude models. These narrower notes still transfer in most settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Priority notes also raise GPT-5.6’s unsafe actions</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure A7 ‣ B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a>). Priority notes formed from “you should prioritize speed here” raise GPT-5.6’s unsafe-action rate on all four comparisons, from 0.18 to 0.30 and 0.15 to 0.31 on the confirmation set and from 0.35 to 0.49 and 0.07 to 0.29 on the self-verification set, while transcripts of the same sessions stay near the no-memory rate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2611.07554v1/gpt56_priority_profile.svg" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/209;" width="548" height="209"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A7: </span>GPT-5.6 priority formation and benign profiles. (a) Unsafe-action rates with no memory, a transcript of the priority session, or the priority note. (b) Unsafe-action rate on the confirmation set for permission notes formed with benign profiles of increasing length in memory.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Limitations and future work</h2>

<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our settings are designed to probe for this failure. The formation interactions are scripted, the autonomy scenarios and broken-safeguard defects are constructed, and each deployment task lies outside the memory’s scope by design. The tasks therefore isolate the trap but do not measure how often it occurs in deployment. The settings are also simplified: high-stakes actions are simulated, and memory comes from short scripted sessions and is loaded automatically without retrieval, while deployed agents retrieve from many memories built up over long histories with real users. Five of our six models come from one family, and GPT-5.6, the only model from another, appears only in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Additional model results ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. Newer models and other model families may behave differently. Finally, agents overgeneralize even from raw transcripts, most on multi-step batch tasks, and our experiments do not yet explain why. Batch tasks differ from single-item tasks in several features, and it remains open whether this generalization arises in the agent’s reasoning at deployment, from task structure, or from other factors such as transcript length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future work.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results suggest several mitigations. First, models could be fine-tuned to write memory that preserves scope: a note can record the task, the occasion, and who granted a permission, instead of turning a one-time request into a standing rule. Second, agent harnesses could guard against the memory trap, for example by storing each memory with its scope and asking the user before a remembered preference licenses an irreversible action. Third, models could be trained to treat remembered preferences as evidence rather than instructions when the stakes are high. Any mitigation should be audited by behavior, not only by text, because notes that read as narrow still cause unsafe actions (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Scope audit. ‣ B.1 Autonomy ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). Extending these tests to deployed memory systems with real users and long histories is a natural next step, including agents that share memory across users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impact.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Persistent memory is becoming a standard feature of language agents, and agents often write it themselves. Our results show that the resulting risk needs no attacker and no false memory: ordinary requests and correct procedures are enough. Safety evaluations of agents with memory should therefore include tasks outside the scope of what the agent remembers, and our three settings, spanning autonomy, feedback, and cheating, offer a starting point for such evaluations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional related work</h2>

<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sycophancy and reward hacking.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work studies sycophancy under preference training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and truthfulness under preference optimization and hindsight feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. We test whether remembered preferences bias later evaluations toward praise or criticism. For coding agents, ImpossibleBench measures specification-violating behavior on conflicting SWE-bench tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Other studies examine chain-of-thought monitoring, training on gameable environments, and environmental feedback loops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denison et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our coding experiments test whether agents reuse a legitimate test-repair procedure where that repair conceals a defect, with model weights held fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization and autonomy.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Goal misgeneralization and emergent misalignment concern behavior beyond the training setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Di Langosco et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Here, generalization happens through memory. Agent benchmarks measure task completion and refusal of harmful requests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; uncertainty-aware planning examines when to ask for help <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our autonomy tasks test whether memory suppresses needed checks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human memory.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transcripts and notes parallel episodic and semantic memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sumers et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and human memory also overgeneralizes. Generalization from one experience to new ones is graded by similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shepard, 1987</a>)</cite>; fear learned in one situation spreads to safe ones in anxiety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dunsmoor and Paz, 2015</a>)</cite>; overgeneral autobiographical memory accompanies emotional disorders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams et al., 2007</a>)</cite>; and gist-based memory produces false recall of related items <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roediger and McDermott, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brainerd and Reyna, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schacter et al., 2011</a>)</cite>. A note can likewise keep a preference or procedure but lose the conditions that limited it. Unlike misinformation effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loftus, 2005</a>)</cite>, the memory trap can arise from an accurate record.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Qualitative examples</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autonomy</span> (Figures <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure A8 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a>–<a href="#A5.F13" title="Figure A13 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A13</span></a>). Each figure shows a note saved after a low-stakes chore, then a high-stakes task run 10 times without memory and 10 times with the note. Figure <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure A8 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A8</span></a> shows a payment task. Figure <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure A9 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A9</span></a> shows a Sonnet-4.6 priority note on a coding task. Figures <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure A10 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A10</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure A12 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A12</span></a> show Sonnet-4.6 permission notes on everyday-life tasks, and Figures <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure A11 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A11</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F13" title="Figure A13 ‣ Appendix E Qualitative examples ‣ The Memory Trap: Language Agents Overgeneralize from Benign Interactions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A13</span></a> show Opus-4.7 permission notes on coding tasks. Without memory, the agent never takes the unsafe action: it checks with the user, raises the risk it found, or runs the optional check that reveals the problem. With the note, it takes the unsafe action as its first or second tool call in all 10 episodes of each task. Italic text is our description of the outcome; in all examples, <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D65F00;">orange text</span> marks overgeneralized note content and <span id="A5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C8102E;">red text</span> the resulting unsafe reasoning or action.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="372.24" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 372.24" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,372.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.74 L 0 367.5 C 0 370.12 2.12 372.24 4.74 372.24 L 545.26 372.24 C 547.88 372.24 550 370.12 550 367.5 L 550 4.74 C 550 2.12 547.88 0 545.26 0 L 4.74 0 C 2.12 0 0 2.12 0 4.74 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 4.74 L 1.97 353.16 L 548.03 353.16 L 548.03 4.74 C 548.03 3.21 546.79 1.97 545.26 1.97 L 4.74 1.97 C 3.21 1.97 1.97 3.21 1.97 4.74 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.2 357.9)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="533.56"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
